{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7982171,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"gardening\",\"short_title\":\"seed-saving-beginners-store-vegetable-seeds\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-08-08 18:14:17\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-08-08 18:14:17\",\"author\":\"David Park\",\"img\":null,\"title\":\"Seed Saving for Beginners: How to Store Vegetable Seeds\",\"description\":\"Learn how to save and store vegetable seeds at home. Master dry and wet processing methods for long-term seed viability.\",\"url\":null,\"body\":\"<h2>Why Save Your Own Seeds?</h2>\\n<p>Saving seeds from your garden preserves heirloom varieties, ensures plants adapted to your local climate, and eliminates annual seed costs. A single packet of commercial tomato seeds costs $3 to $5; saving seeds from one healthy plant can provide enough for 5-10 years of plantings. The key is knowing which plants produce true-to-type seeds and how to process and store them properly.</p>\\n<h2>Open-Pollinated vs Hybrid Seeds</h2>\\n<p>Only save seeds from open-pollinated and heirloom varieties. Hybrid seeds (labeled F1) produce unpredictable offspring — the next generation may lack the desirable traits of the parent plant. Open-pollinated varieties breed true when isolated from other varieties of the same species.</p>\\n<h3>Self-Pollinating (Easiest to Save)</h3>\\n<p>Tomatoes, beans, peas, and lettuce self-pollinate within the flower, so cross-contamination is rare. Simply let the fruit fully ripen on the plant, extract seeds, dry, and store. These are the best starting point for beginners.</p>\\n<h3>Cross-Pollinating (Require Isolation)</h3>\\n<p>Squash, corn, cucumbers, and brassicas need insect or wind pollination between plants. If two varieties of the same species grow nearby, they will cross-pollinate. Save pure seed by spacing varieties at least 100-1,000 feet apart or using physical barriers like mesh bags over flowers.</p>\\n<h2>Seed Processing Methods</h2>\\n<h3>Dry Processing (Beans, Peas, Lettuce, Flowers)</h3>\\n<p>Let seed pods dry completely on the plant until they rattle. Harvest pods, thresh them to separate seeds, and winnow by pouring between bowls in a light breeze — chaff blows away while seeds fall through.</p>\\n<h3>Wet Processing (Tomatoes, Cucumbers, Squash)</h3>\\n<p>Scoop seeds with their surrounding gel into a jar, add water, and ferment for 2-3 days at room temperature. This fermentation kills seed-borne diseases and removes the germination-inhibiting gel coating. Rinse seeds thoroughly, spread on a paper towel, and dry for 1-2 weeks.</p>\\n<h2>Proper Seed Storage</h2>\\n<p>Seeds need cool, dry, and dark conditions. Ideal storage is 32-41°F with less than 8 percent seed moisture content. Store dried seeds in labeled paper envelopes inside airtight glass jars or plastic containers in the refrigerator. Add a desiccant packet to absorb residual moisture. Properly stored seeds remain viable for:</p>\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Tomatoes, peppers, beans, peas</strong>: 4-6 years</li>\\n<li><strong>Lettuce, squash, cucumbers</strong>: 3-5 years</li>\\n<li><strong>Carrots, onions, spinach</strong>: 1-2 years</li>\\n</ul>\\n<h2>Viability Testing</h2>\\n<p>Before planting season, test stored seeds by placing 10 seeds on a damp paper towel, rolling it up, and keeping it warm for 5-10 days. Count germinated seeds to calculate the germination rate. If fewer than 50 percent sprout, replace your seed stock.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":null,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-08 18:14:17\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}